Dear Tim,

yes, the data you sketch might be amenable to an IPM. But you wouldn’t have to buy our entire book (*), reading the Richard Chandler & Clark bear paper in MEE in 2014 would be the ideal start. And then think about how to merge the info from the other data with the SCR data.

Best regards  -- Marc

(*) Although you would probably still gain some insights from reading at least parts of our book ….

Von: hmec...@googlegroups.com <hmec...@googlegroups.com> Im Auftrag von Tim Henderson
Gesendet: Dienstag, 30. November 2021 08:01
An: hmecology: Hierarchical Modeling in Ecology <hmec...@googlegroups.com>
Betreff: Re: The new IPM textbook is out now !

Hi Marc,

Would this book be useful for someone with a large camera trapping data set looking at an species with unique markings (spots). I'm currently reviewing ways to approach this data (e.g. SECR) to determine density, population dynamics and potentially PVAs. In addition to the camera data, I have some demographic data (age & sex) which was collected during the camera surveys. I am new to IPMs and would be interested in pursing this if it's appropriate for my research.

Kind regards,

Tim

